Introduction to biology: Biology studies life from molecules to ecosystems; scientific method drives discovery; interdisciplinary connections with physics, chemistry, and earth science.

Introductory microbiology: Bacteria (prokaryotes) and viruses (acellular) are foundational to life sciences; Gram stain differentiates bacteria; biotechnology applications transform medicine and industry.

Vegetation: Nepal's altitude-dependent vegetation zones reflect climate gradients; in-situ (protected areas) and ex-situ (seed banks) conservation preserve plant diversity.</td></tr><tr><td>18: Chemical properties of ammonia (action with CuSO4 solutio…</td><td>Chemical properties of ammonia (action with CuSO4 solutio… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Chemical properties of ammonia, action with CuSO4 solution, water. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>19: Phosphorus and Sulphur</td><td>Atomic structure: From Rutherford's nuclear model through Bohr's quantized orbits to the modern quantum mechanical model, understanding electron configuration using four quantum numbers is fundamental.

Stoichiometry: The mole concept bridges atomic and macroscopic scales; limiting reagent determines theoretical yield; concentration expressed as molarity or molality.

Chemical equilibrium: Dynamic equilibrium has equal forward and backward rates; Kc/Kp are temperature-dependent; Le Chatelier's principle predicts response to stress.

Thermodynamics: First law (energy conservation); enthalpy changes in reactions; Gibbs free energy determines spontaneity; entropy of universe always increases.

Oxidation-reduction: Redox involves electron transfer; oxidation numbers track electron flow; electrochemical cells convert chemical to electrical energy; Faraday's laws quantify electrolysis.

States of matter: Gas laws relate P, V, T, n; kinetic theory explains gas behavior; real gases deviate due to intermolecular forces and molecular volume.

Solutions: Molarity and molality express concentration; colligative properties depend on particle number; Raoult's law describes vapor pressure of ideal solutions.

Chemical bonding: Ionic bonds form from electron transfer; covalent bonds from sharing; VSEPR predicts molecular geometry; hybridization explains bonding orbitals.

Organic chemistry basics: Carbon's tetravalency and catenation enable millions of compounds; functional groups determine reactivity; IUPAC nomenclature provides systematic naming.

Hydrocarbons: Alkanes (CnH2n+2) are saturated; alkenes (CnH2n) have C=C; alkynes (CnH2n-2) have C≡C; aromatic compounds based on benzene ring.

Periodic table: Elements arranged by atomic number; periodic trends in radius, ionization energy, electronegativity; s, p, d, f blocks based on orbital type.

Applied chemistry: Petrochemical industry, polymer science, green chemistry principles; importance of sustainable chemical manufacturing for environmental protection.</td></tr><tr><td>20: Oxy-acids of nitrogen (name and formula)</td><td>Oxy-acids of nitrogen (name and formula) (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Oxy-acids of nitrogen, name, formula. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>21: Chemical properties of nitric acid: HNO3 as an acid and o…</td><td>Chemical properties of nitric acid: HNO3 as an acid and o… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Chemical properties of nitric acid: HNO3 as an acid, oxidizing agent, action with zinc. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>22: Halogens: general characteristics of halogens; comparativ…</td><td>Halogens: general characteristics of halogens; comparativ… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Halogens, general characteristics of halogens, comparative study on preparation. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>23: Test for Cl2, Br2 and I2</td><td>Test for Cl2, Br2 and I2 (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Test for Cl2, Br2 and I2. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>24: Haloacids (HCl, HBr and HI): comparative study on prepara…</td><td>Haloacids (HCl, HBr and HI): comparative study on prepara… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Haloacids, : comparative study on preparation, properties. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>25: Carbon: allotropes of carbon (crystalline and amorphous) …</td><td>Carbon: allotropes of carbon (crystalline and amorphous) … (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Carbon, allotropes of carbon, crystalline. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>26: Properties (reducing action, reaction with metals and non…</td><td>Properties (reducing action, reaction with metals and non… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Properties, reducing action, reaction with metals. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>27: Phosphorus: allotropes of phosphorus (name only)</td><td>Phosphorus: allotropes of phosphorus (name only) (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Phosphorus, allotropes of phosphorus, name only. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>28: Phosphine: preparation, properties (basic nature, reducin…</td><td>Phosphine: preparation, properties (basic nature, reducin… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Phosphine, preparation, properties. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>29: Sulphur: allotropes of sulphur (name only) and uses of su…</td><td>Sulphur: allotropes of sulphur (name only) and uses of su… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Sulphur, allotropes of sulphur, name only. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>30: Hydrogen sulphide: preparation from Kipp's apparatus (wit…</td><td>Hydrogen sulphide: preparation from Kipp's apparatus (wit… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Hydrogen sulphide, preparation from Kipp's apparatus, diagram. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>31: Sulphur dioxide: properties (acidic nature, reducing natu…</td><td>Sulphur dioxide: properties (acidic nature, reducing natu… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Sulphur dioxide, properties, acidic nature. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>32: Sulphuric acid: properties (acidic nature, oxidising natu…</td><td>Sulphuric acid: properties (acidic nature, oxidising natu… (Chemistry of Non-metals, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Sulphuric acid, properties, acidic nature. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>33: Sodium thiosulphate (formula and uses)</td><td>Non-metals: electronegative, form anions.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Chemistry Of Non Metals.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Chemical properties arise from the arrangement and interaction of electrons and nuclei. Conservation of atoms and charge constrains reactions; molecular structure and energy changes help explain which products form.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Treat oxidation state, bonding diagrams and reaction equations as models with defined rules. Temperature, pressure, solvent, concentration, particle size and catalysts can alter rates or equilibria; name the conditions whenever comparing results.</p><h3>Open research question — How chemistry led to the first living systems</h3><p>Life’s existence and subsequent evolution are established, but the detailed sequence by which non-living chemistry produced self-sustaining living systems remains unresolved. Researchers investigate plausible molecules and environments without treating any single pathway as proven.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Chemistry Of Non Metals”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “How chemistry led to the first living systems”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-1-chemical-equilibria">OpenStax Chemistry: chemical equilibrium</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-5-periodic-variations-in-element-properties">OpenStax Chemistry: periodic trends</a></li><li><a href="https://astrobiology.nasa.gov/research/">NASA Astrobiology: research questions</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>